General elections were held in Haiti in 1995 for the [[President of Haiti|presidency]] and for seats in the [[Chamber of Deputies (Haiti)|Chamber of Deputies]] and the [[Senate (Haiti)|Senate]]. The presidential election, held on 17 December, resulted in a victory for [[René Préval]] of [[Fanmi Lavalas]].<ref>[[Dieter Nohlen]] (2005) ''Elections in the Americas: A data handbook, Volume I'', p392 {{ISBN|978-0-19-928357-6}}</ref> The parliamentary elections, held on 25 June, 13 August and 17 September, were also won by Lavalas. Voter turnout was just 31.09% for the parliamentary elections and 27.8% for the presidential elections.<ref>[http://www.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/arc/2137_95.htm Haiti: Elections held in 1995] Inter-Parliamentary Union</ref><ref>Nohlen, p392</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |date=1996 |title=Election Watch |url=https://muse.jhu.edu/pub/1/article/697485 |journal=Journal of Democracy |volume=7 |issue=2 |issn=1086-3214}}</ref>

Préval was inaugurated as president of Haiti on 7 February 1996, which was the first peaceful transition of power from one elected Haitian president to another.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Metz |first=Helen Chapin |title=Dominican Republic and Haiti: Country Studies |publisher=Federal Research Division, Library of Congress |location=Washington, D.C. |year=2001 |url=https://www.loc.gov/item/2001023524/ |page=309 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |first1=Sasha |last1=Filippova |first2=Kristina |last2=Fried |first3=Brian |last3=Concannon |title=From coup to chaos: 20 years after the US ousted Haiti's president |work=[[Quincy Institute for Responsible Statecraft]] |date=1 March 2024 |access-date=14 November 2025 |url=https://responsiblestatecraft.org/us-haiti-coup/ }}</ref>

==Background==
The 25 June 1995 parliamentary elections were the first to be held in Haiti since the return of President [[Jean-Bertrand Aristide]] during [[Operation Uphold Democracy]] in 1994. In the parliamentary elections, around 11,000 candidates ran for 700 seats, including in the [[National Assembly (Haiti)|National Assembly]] and local and municipal councils.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Haiti elections |date=23 May 1994 |access-date=3 January 2026 |work=UPI |url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1995/05/23/Haiti-elections/5265801201600/ }}</ref> In total, 2,200 political offices were contested during the general election, and 3.4 million of the four million eligible voters in Haiti were registered.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bazinet |first=Kenneth R. |title=U.S. sending observer to Haiti elections |date=23 June 1995 |access-date=3 January 2026 |work=UPI |url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1995/06/23/US-sending-observer-to-Haiti-elections/7015803880000/ }}</ref> There were relatively few violent incidents during the campaign compared to past elections in Haiti, leading to free and fair elections, according to the [[Organization of American States|OAS]] observer mission.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Officials split on Haiti vote conditions |date=24 June 1995 |access-date=3 January 2026 |work=UPI |url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1995/06/24/Officials-split-on-Haiti-vote-conditions/8883803966400/ }}</ref>

